Updating arrays → Add, remove, or control array elements.

a)$push — Add an Element

$push adds an element to the end of an array.

Suppose:

skills: ["javascript", "react"]

Run:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: "mongodb"
        }
    }
);

Result:

skills: [
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "mongodb"
]
Important

$push allows duplicates.

If react already exists:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: "react"
        }
    }
);

Result:

skills: [
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "mongodb",
    "react"
]
-------------------------------------
b). $addToSet — Add Without Duplicates

$addToSet adds an element only if it doesn't already exist.

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $addToSet: {
            skills: "react"
        }
    }
);

If react already exists:

Nothing is added.

If mongodb doesn't exist:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $addToSet: {
            skills: "mongodb"
        }
    }
);

MongoDB adds it.

$push vs $addToSet
Operator	Duplicate allowed?
$push	✅ Yes
$addToSet	❌ No
Easy memory trick
$push
→ Just add

$addToSet
→ Add to the SET
→ Sets don't contain duplicates
-------------------------------------------------
c) $each — Add Multiple Elements

$each is used with $push or $addToSet when you want to add multiple elements.

Example:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: {
                $each: [
                    "node",
                    "express",
                    "mongodb"
                ]
            }
        }
    }
);

If before:

skills: ["javascript", "react"]

After:

skills: [
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "node",
    "express",
    "mongodb"
]
$each + $addToSet

You can also prevent duplicates:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $addToSet: {
            skills: {
                $each: [
                    "node",
                    "express",
                    "react"
                ]
            }
        }
    }
);

If react already exists, it won't be added again.
-----------------------------
d). $position — Choose Where to Insert

$position controls the array index where $push inserts elements.

Suppose:

skills: [
    "javascript",  // index 0
    "react",       // index 1
    "mongodb",     // index 2
    "node"         // index 3
]
Insert at Beginning
$position: 0

Example:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: {
                $each: ["typescript"],
                $position: 0
            }
        }
    }
);

Result:

[
    "typescript",
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "mongodb",
    "node"
]
Insert in the Middle

To insert before index 2:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: {
                $each: ["typescript"],
                $position: 2
            }
        }
    }
);

Result:

[
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "typescript",
    "mongodb",
    "node"
]
Important

$position means:

Insert before this index.

It does NOT mean:

"Put it in the middle."

Insert at End

Normally you don't need $position.

Just use:

$push: {
    skills: "typescript"
}

$push naturally adds to the end.
---------------------------------------
e) $slice — Limit Array Elements

$slice controls how many elements should remain in the array after $push.

This is usually used together with $push.

Suppose:

skills: [
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "node"
]

Run:

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            skills: {
                $each: ["mongodb"],
                $slice: 3
            }
        }
    }
);
Step 1 — $push

MongoDB first adds:

[
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "node",
    "mongodb"
]
Step 2 — $slice: 3

Keep only the first 3:

[
    "javascript",
    "react",
    "node"
]

So mongodb gets removed again.

Positive $slice
$slice: 3

Keep the first 3.

[A, B, C, D, E]

↓

[A, B, C]
Negative $slice
$slice: -3

Keep the last 3.

[A, B, C, D, E]

↓

[C, D, E]
Very useful example

Keep only the latest 5 messages:

db.users.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $push: {
            messages: {
                $each: ["new message"],
                $slice: -5
            }
        }
    }
);

This keeps the array at a maximum of 5 recent messages.
--------------------------
f). $pop — Remove First or Last Element

$pop removes exactly one element.

Remove last element
db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $pop: {
            skills: 1
        }
    }
);
1 → remove last
Remove first element
db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $pop: {
            skills: -1
        }
    }
);
-1 → remove first
Important

You cannot use:

$pop: 2

to remove two elements.

Remember:

$pop: 1
→ remove ONE from end

$pop: -1
→ remove ONE from beginning
-------------------------------------------------
g). $pull — Remove Based on Value/Condition

$pull removes array elements matching a value or condition.

Suppose:

skills: [
    "react",
    "node",
    "mongodb"
]

Remove "react":

db.employees.updateOne(
    {name: "tayyab"},
    {
        $pull: {
            skills: "react"
        }
    }
);

Result:

[
    "node",
    "mongodb"
]
$pull with a Condition

Suppose:

scores: [30, 50, 80, 20, 90]

Remove all scores below 50:

db.students.updateOne(
    {name: "Ali"},
    {
        $pull: {
            scores: {
                $lt: 50
            }
        }
    }
);

Result:

[
    50,
    80,
    90
]

So $pull can remove multiple matching elements.

$pop vs $pull

This is an important difference.

$pop

Position-based:

$pop: {
    skills: 1
}

Means:

Remove the last element.

$pull

Value/condition-based:

$pull: {
    skills: "react"
}

Means:

Remove matching elements.

Remember
$pop
→ WHERE?
→ Beginning / End

$pull
→ WHICH VALUE?
→ Based on value/condition
